  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Gusty Winds and Choppy Lakes Today\n\n-  Periodic Showers Through Tuesday, Limited Winter Impacts\n\n-  Mid-Week: Inversions and Fog Return\n\nToday through Tuesday: A weak cold front will drop into the \nNorthern Rockies this morning, initiating scattered showers across\nnorthwest Montana and north-central Idaho. This precipitation \nwill translate southward into west-central Montana this afternoon\nwhile gradually tapering off. Snow levels will hover between \n5,500 and 6,500 feet, precluding any significant winter weather \nimpacts. Breezy westerly winds will develop along and behind the \nfrontal passage, with valley gusts of 20 to 30 mph generating \nchoppy conditions on area lakes. These winds are expected to \ndecouple and diminish this evening.\n\nWhile a few light showers may linger into early Monday, a more \norganized, widespread wave of precipitation will traverse the \nregion from Monday evening through Tuesday afternoon. Overall \naccumulations will remain relatively light. With snow levels \nremaining elevated between 6,000 and 7,000 feet, travel impacts \nwill once again be minimal.\n\nWednesday through Late Next Week: A dominant upper-level ridge is\nforecast to build over the region starting Wednesday, persisting \nthrough the remainder of the workweek. This pattern will foster \nstrong radiational cooling at night and the development of \npronounced valley inversions. Consequently, areas of fog and low \nstratus are probable, if these inversions become entrenched, they\nwill limit diurnal heating, likely keeping valley high \ntemperatures noticeably cooler than full-mixing model guidance \nsuggests.\n\nLooking toward the end of the forecast period, ensemble guidance \nindicates the ridge axis migrates eastward. This will open the \ndoor to a more progressive southwest flow, reintroducing \nprecipitation chances and effectively scouring out any lingering \nvalley inversions.",
